<title>Exam 2, Spring 2026</title>

<!-- Include the Exam Instructions file as the first page -->
<xi:include href="../exam-instructions.ptx" />

<page>
<title>True / False</title>
<problem>
    <introduction>
        <p>
            (8 points) Suppose <m>A</m> is an <m>m \times n</m> matrix and <m>\vec b\in \mathbb R ^{m}</m> unless otherwise stated. Select <em>true</em> if the statement is true for all choices of <m>A</m> and <m>\vec b</m>.  Otherwise, select <em>false</em>.
        </p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="true">
    <statement>
        <p>
            <em>True &#160;  / &#160;  False</em>: &#160; If <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> are row equivalent, then <m>\det (A) = \det (B)</m>.
        </p>
    </statement>
    <hint>
        <p>
            Do row operations change the determinant?
        </p>
    </hint>
    <solution>
        <p>
            <em> False </em>. Let <m> A = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix}</m> and <m> B = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 2 \end{bmatrix}</m>. Then <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> are row equivalent, but <m>\det(A) = 1</m> and <m>\det(B) = 2</m>.
        </p>
    </solution>
    </task>

    <task workspace="true">
        <statement>
            <p>
                <em>True &#160;  / &#160;  False</em>: &#160; If <m>\vec{p}</m> and <m> \vec{q} </m> are probability vectors in <m>\mathbb R ^3</m>, then <m>\frac{1}{3} \vec{p}  + \frac{2}{3} \vec{q}</m> is also a probability vector in <m>\mathbb R ^3</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <hint>
            <p>
                Recall that a probability vector in <m>\mathbb R ^3</m> is a vector with nonnegative entries that sum to 1.
            </p>
        </hint>
        <solution>
            <p>
                <em> True </em>. Since <m>\vec{p}</m> and <m> \vec{q} </m> are probability vectors, their entries are nonnegative and sum to 1. The linear combination <m>\frac{1}{3} \vec{p}  + \frac{2}{3} \vec{q}</m> will also have nonnegative entries and the sum of its entries will be <m>\frac{1}{3} + \frac{2}{3} = 1</m>, making it a probability vector.
            </p>    
        </solution>
    </task>

    <task workspace="true">
        <statement>
            <p>
                <em>True &#160;  / &#160;  False</em>: &#160; If the matrix product <m> AB </m> is defined, then the product <m>BA</m> is also defined.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <hint>
            <p>
                For a matrix product to be defined, the number of columns of the first matrix must equal the number of rows of the second matrix.
            </p>
        </hint>
        <solution>
            <p>
                <em> False </em>. Consider <m> A = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \end{bmatrix} </m> and <m> B = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix} </m>. Then <m> AB = [1] </m> is defined, but <m> BA </m> is not defined (The number of columns of <m> A </m> does not equal the number of rows of <m> B </m>).
            </p>
        </solution>
    </task>

    <task workspace="true">
        <statement>
            <p>
                <em>True &#160;  / &#160;  False</em>: &#160; If <m>\det (A) = 0</m>, then <m>A</m> must have a zero row or have two rows which are identical.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <hint>
            <p>
                <m> \det (A) = 0 </m> when <m> A </m> is not invertible.
            </p>
        </hint>
        <solution>
            <p>
                <em> False </em>. Consider <m> A = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 2 \\ 3 \amp 6 \end{bmatrix} </m>. Then <m> \det(A) = 0 </m>, but <m> A </m> does not have a zero row and does not have two identical rows.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </task>

    <task workspace="true">
        <statement>
            <p>
                <em>True &#160;  / &#160;  False</em>: &#160; If <m>A</m> is an <m>n \times n</m> matrix and <m>A = LU</m> is an <m>LU</m> factorization of <m>A</m>, then <m>\det(A) = \det(U)</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <hint>
            <p>
                Use the multiplicity of the determinant.
            </p>
        </hint>
        <solution>
            <p>
                <em> True </em>. Since <m> A = LU </m>, we have <m> \det(A) = \det(LU) </m>. By the multiplicity of the determinant, this becomes <m> \det(A) = \det(L) \det(U) </m>. The matrix <m> L </m> is lower triangular with ones on the diagonal, so <m> \det(L) = 1 </m>. Therefore, <m> \det(A) = \det(U) </m>.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </task>

    <task workspace="true">
        <statement>
            <p>
                <em>True &#160;  / &#160;  False</em>: &#160; If <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> are invertible <m>n \times n</m> matrices then <m>A + B</m> is also invertible.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <hint>
            <p>
                Play around with some <m> 2 \times 2 </m> examples.
            </p>
        </hint>
        <solution>
            <p>
                <em> False </em>. Consider <m> A = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix} </m> and <m> B = \begin{bmatrix} -1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp -1 \end{bmatrix} </m>. Then <m> A </m> and <m> B </m> are both invertible, but <m> A + B = \begin{bmatrix} 0 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \end{bmatrix} </m> is not invertible.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </task>

    <task workspace="true">
        <statement>
            <p>
                <em>True &#160;  / &#160;  False</em>: &#160; If <m> A </m> is an <m> n \times n </m> matrix and <m>c</m> is a scalar such that <m>\det(A - cI_n) = 0</m>, then there exists a nonzero vector <m>\vec{v} \in \mathbb R ^n</m> such that <m>A \vec{v} \in \text{span}\{\vec{v} \}</m>
            </p>
        </statement>
        <hint>
            <p>
                Relate everything to eigenvectors.
            </p>
        </hint>
        <solution>
            <p>
                <em> True </em>. The condition <m> \det(A - cI_n) = 0 </m> means that <m> c </m> is an eigenvalue of <m> A </m>. By the definition of eigenvectors, there exists a nonzero vector <m> \vec{v} \in \mathbb R^n </m> such that <m> A \vec{v} = c \vec{v} </m>. Therefore, <m> A \vec{v} \in \text{span}\{\vec{v} \} </m>.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </task>

    <task workspace="true">
        <statement>
            <p>
                <em>True &#160;  / &#160;  False</em>: &#160; If <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> are both <m>n \times n</m> matrices with pivots in each of their columns, then the product <m>AB</m> has a pivot in each of its columns
            </p>
        </statement>
        <hint>
            <p>
                Use the Invertible Matrix Theorem
            </p>
        </hint>
        <solution>
            <p>
                <em> True </em>. By the Invertible Matrix Theorem, if <m> A </m> and <m> B </m> have pivots in each of their columns, then both <m> A </m> and <m> B </m> are invertible (<m> A,B </m> are squre, so IMT applies). The product of two invertible matrices is also invertible, and therefore <m> AB </m> has a pivot in each of its columns.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </task>
    
</problem>


</page>


<page>
<problem>
    <introduction>
        <p>
           (4 points) Indicate whether the following situations are possible or impossible. 
        </p>
    </introduction>

    <task workspace="0.5in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                <em>Possible &#160;  / &#160;  Impossible</em>: &#160; A <m>2 \times 2</m> matrix <m>A</m> with <m>\text{dim}(\Nul(A)) = 0</m> such that the system <m>A \vec{x} = \begin{bmatrix}1 \\ 2\end{bmatrix}</m> has no solution.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <hint>
            <p>
                Use the Rank-Nullity Theorem
            </p>
        </hint>
        <solution>
            <p>
                <em> Impossible </em>. By the Rank-Nullity Theorem, if <m> \text{dim}(\Nul(A)) = 0 </m>, then <m> \rank(A) = 2 </m>. Since <m> A </m> is <m> 2 \times 2 </m>, <m> A </m> is invertible, and thus <m> A </m> is both onto and one-to-one. Equivalently, system <m> A \vec{x} = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 2 \end{bmatrix} </m> must have a solution, and such a solution must be unique.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </task>

    <task workspace="0.5in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                <em>Possible &#160;  / &#160;  Impossible</em>: &#160; A <m>3 \times 5</m> matrix <m>A</m> so that <m>\rank(A) = 4</m>
            </p>
        </statement>
        <hint>
            <p>
                Play around with some examples of <m> 3 \times 5 </m> matrices and their ranks.
            </p>
        </hint>
        <solution>
            <p>
                <em> Impossible </em>. The maximum rank of a <m> 3 \times 5 </m> matrix is <m> 3 </m> (the minimum of the number of rows and columns), so it cannot have rank <m> 4 </m>.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </task>

    <task workspace="0.5in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                <em>Possible &#160;  / &#160;  Impossible</em>: &#160; Two <m>2 \times 2</m> non-zero matrices A and B, neither of which is <m>I_2</m>, with <m>AB = BA</m>
            </p>
        </statement>
        <hint>
            <p>
                Try some diagonal matrices.
            </p>
        </hint>
        <solution>
            <p>
                <em> Possible </em>. For example, let <m> A = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 2 \end{bmatrix} </m> and <m> B = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 3 \end{bmatrix} </m>. Then <m> AB = BA </m>, and neither <m> A </m> nor <m> B </m> is <m> I_2 </m>.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </task>

    <task workspace="0.5in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                <em>Possible &#160;  / &#160;  Impossible</em>: &#160; A <m>2 \times 2</m> invertible matrix <m>A = \begin{bmatrix}a \amp b \\ c \amp d\end{bmatrix}</m> so that <m>\det (A) = \det\begin{pmatrix}a \amp b \\ 2a + c \amp 2b + d\end{pmatrix}</m>
            </p>
        </statement>
        <hint>
            <p>
                Consider the effect of row operations on the determinant.
            </p>
        </hint>
        <solution>
            <p>
                <em> Possible </em>. For example, let <m> A = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix} </m>. Then <m> \det(A) = 1 </m> and <m> \det\begin{pmatrix}1 \amp 0 \\ 2 \amp 1\end{pmatrix} = 1 </m>, so the condition is satisfied. In fact, any invertible matrix will work, since row replacement operations do not change the determinant.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </task>


</problem>

<p workspace="0.5in">
    
</p>

<problem workspace="3in">
    <statement>
        <p>        
            (2 points) Let <m>A</m> be an <m>n \times n</m> matrix such that for every <m>\vec{b} \in \mathbb R ^n</m>, the system <m>A \vec{x} = \vec{b}</m> has a unique solution. Which of the following statements is FALSE?

            <m>\textit{Select only one}</m>
        </p>
        
        <ol marker="(a)">
            <li>The rows of <m>A</m> form a basis for <m>\mathbb R ^n</m></li>
            <li>dim <m>\{ \vec{x} \mid A \vec{x} = \vec{0} \} > 0</m>.</li>
            <li>The system <m>A^T \vec{x} = \vec{b}</m> has a unique solution for every <m>\vec{b} \in \mathbb R^n</m></li>
            <li>The columns of <m>A</m> are linearly independent.</li>
        </ol>

    </statement>
    <hint>
        <p>
            Notice that <m> A </m> is square, so use the Invertible Matrix Theorem.
        </p>
    </hint>
    <solution>
        <p>
            Option (b) is the only correct answer.
        </p>
        <p>
            Option (a) is a true statement. Since <m> A </m> is square and <m> A\vec{x} = \vec{b} </m> has a unique solution for every <m> \vec{b} \in \mathbb R^n </m>, <m> A </m> is one-to-one (unique) and onto (always has a solution). Equivalently, <m> A </m> is invertible. So <m> A </m> has a pivot in every row, and thus the rows of <m> A </m> are linearly independent. Any <m> n </m> linearly independent vectors in <m> \R^n </m> form a basis for <m> \R^n </m>, so the rows of <m> A </m> form a basis for <m> \R^n </m>.
        </p>
        <p>
            Option (b) is a false statement. Since <m> A </m> is invertible, the only solution to <m> A \vec{x} = \vec{0} </m> is <m> \vec{x} = \vec{0} </m>, so the dimension of the solution space to <m> A \vec{x} = \vec{0} </m> is 0.
        </p>
        <p>
            Option (c) is a true statement. Since <m> A </m> is invertible, <m> A^T </m> is also invertible, and thus the system <m> A^T \vec{x} = \vec{b} </m> has a unique solution for every <m> \vec{b} \in \mathbb R^n </m>.
        </p>
        <p>
            Option (d) is a true statement. Since <m> A </m> is invertible, the columns of <m> A </m> are linearly independent.
        </p>
    </solution>
</problem>
    
</page>

<page>
    <problem workspace="3in">
    <statement>
        <p>        
            (2 points) Which of the following subsets of <m>\mathbb R ^3</m> are subspaces?

            <m>\textit{Select all that apply}</m>
        </p>
        
        <ol marker="(a)">
            <li><m>S_1 = \{ (x,y,z) \in \mathbb R^3 : x-2y+z = 1 \}</m></li>
            <li><m>S_2 = \{ s(1,0,1) + t(0,1,1) : s,t \in \mathbb R \}</m></li>
            <li><m>S_3 = \{ (x,y,z) \in \mathbb R^3 : xy=0 \}</m></li>
            <li><m>S_4 = \{ (x,y,z) \in \mathbb R^3 : x-2y+z = 0 \}</m></li>
        </ol>

    </statement>
    <hint>
        <p>
            Every subspace can be written as the span of a collection of vectors, and vice versa. A set of vectors that is not a span will not be closed under addition and/or scalar multiplication.
        </p>
    </hint>
    <solution>
        <p>
            Options (b) and (d) are correct.
        </p>
        <p>
            Option (a) is incorrect because <m> \vec{0} \notin S_1 </m>.
        </p>
        <p>
            Option (b) is correct because <m> S_2 </m> is the span of the vectors <m> (1,0,1) </m> and <m> (0,1,1) </m>.
        </p>
        <p>
            Option (c) is incorrect because <m> S_3 </m> is not closed under addition. For example, <m> (1,0,0) \in S_3 </m> and <m> (0,1,0) \in S_3 </m>, but <m> (1,1,0) \notin S_3 </m>.
        </p>
        <p>
            Option (d) is correct because <m> S_4 = \Nul(\begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp -2 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix}) </m>, and the null space of a matrix is always a subspace.
        </p>
    </solution>
</problem>



    <problem workspace="true">
        <statement>
            <p>
                (3 points) Suppose that <m>\det \begin{pmatrix} a \amp b \\ c \amp d \end{pmatrix} = 2 </m> and <m>\det \begin{pmatrix} a \amp b \\ e \amp f \end{pmatrix} = 5</m> and <m>\det \begin{pmatrix} c \amp d \\ e \amp f \end{pmatrix} = -2</m>. Find the value of the determinant below:
                <md>
                    \det \begin{pmatrix} a \amp 2 \amp b \\ c \amp 0 \amp d \\ e \amp 4 \amp f \end{pmatrix} = \framebox{$\phantom{$\begin{bmatrix} a \amp a \amp a  \\ b  \end{bmatrix}$}$ }
                </md>

            </p>
        </statement>
        <hint>
            <p>
                Use cofactor expansion across the 2nd column.
            </p>
        </hint>
        <solution>
            <p>
                Using cofactor expansion across the 2nd column, we get:
                <md>
                    <mrow>\det \begin{pmatrix} a \amp 2 \amp b \\ c \amp 0 \amp d \\ e \amp 4 \amp f \end{pmatrix} = -2 \det \begin{pmatrix} c \amp d \\ e \amp f \end{pmatrix} + 0 - 4 \det \begin{pmatrix} a \amp b \\ c \amp d \end{pmatrix}</mrow>
                    <mrow> = -2(-2) + 0 - 4(2) = -4</mrow>
                </md>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </problem>
</page>

<page>
    <problem workspace="true">
        <statement>
            <p>    
                (3 points) Compute the area of the parallelogram <m>\mathcal{S}</m> having vertices <m>(-1,1), (0,4), (1,-4), \text{ and } (2,-1)</m>. Then, let <m>T : \mathbb R^2 \to \mathbb R^2</m> be the transformation with standard matrix <m>\begin{pmatrix}3 \amp 2 \\ 5 \amp 1 \end{pmatrix}</m>. Find the area of <m>T(\mathcal{S})</m>
                <sidebyside widths = "50% 50%">
                <p></p>
                <p>
                <md>
                    \text{Area}(\mathcal{S}) = \framebox{$\phantom{$\begin{bmatrix} a \amp a \amp a  \\ b  \end{bmatrix}$}$ }
                </md>
                <md>
                    \text{Area}(T(\mathcal{S})) = \framebox{$\phantom{$\begin{bmatrix} a \amp a \amp a  \\ b  \end{bmatrix}$}$ }
                </md>
                </p>
                </sidebyside>
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                Pick an arbitrary vertex of the parallelogram, say <m>(-1,1)</m>. Let us shift the whole parallogram such that this vertex is at the origin. The new vertices are then <m>(0,0), (1,3), (2,-5), \text{ and } (3,-2)</m>.
                The area of the parallelogram is then given by the absolute value of the determinant of the matrix formed by the two adjacent edge vectors from the origin:
                <md>
                    \text{Area}(\mathcal{S}) = \left| \det \begin{pmatrix} 1 \amp 2 \\ 3 \amp -5 \end{pmatrix} \right| = 11
                </md>
                The transformation <m>T</m> has standard matrix <m>\begin{pmatrix}3 \amp 2 \\ 5 \amp 1 \end{pmatrix}</m>, so the area of the transformed parallelogram is:
                <md>
                    \text{Area}(T(\mathcal{S})) = \left| \det \begin{pmatrix}3 \amp 2 \\ 5 \amp 1 \end{pmatrix} \right| \cdot \text{Area}(\mathcal{S}) = 7 \cdot 11 = 77
                </md>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </problem>

    <problem workspace="1.5in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                (4 points) Consider the system <m>A \vec{x} = \vec{b}</m>, where

                <md>
                    A = \begin{bmatrix} 7 \amp 4 \\ 3 \amp 2 \end{bmatrix} \text{ and } \vec{b} = \begin{bmatrix} 2 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}
                </md>
                
                Let <m>A_1</m> and <m>A_2</m> be as in Cramer's Rule; that is, Let <m>A_i</m> be the matrix <m>A</m> with column <m>i</m> replaced with <m>\vec{b}</m>. Find <m>\det(A), \det(A_1), \det(A_2)</m>, and the solution vector <m>\vec{x}</m> 
            </p>
            <sidebyside widths = "50% 50%">
            <p></p>
            <p>
                <md>
                    \det(A) = \framebox{$\phantom{$\begin{bmatrix} a \amp a \amp a  \\ b  \end{bmatrix}$}$ }
                </md>
                <md>
                    \det(A_1) = \framebox{$\phantom{$\begin{bmatrix} a \amp a \amp a  \\ b  \end{bmatrix}$}$ }
                </md>
                <md>
                    \det(A_2) = \framebox{$\phantom{$\begin{bmatrix} a \amp a \amp a  \\ b  \end{bmatrix}$}$ }
                </md>
                <md>
                    \vec{x} = \framebox{$\phantom{$\begin{bmatrix} a \amp a \amp a  \\ b \\ c \\ d \\ e  \end{bmatrix}$}$ }
                </md>
            </p>
            </sidebyside>
        </statement>
        <hint>
            <p>
                Cramer's Rule states that for a system <m>A \vec{x} = \vec{b}</m> with <m>A</m> invertible, the solution vector <m>\vec{x}</m> can be found using determinants:
                <md>
                    x_i = \frac{\det(A_i)}{\det(A)}
                </md>
            </p>
        </hint>
        <solution>
            <p>
                <md>
                    \det(A) = \begin{vmatrix} 7 \amp 4 \\ 3 \amp 2 \end{vmatrix} = 2
                </md>
                <md>
                    \det(A_1) = \begin{vmatrix} 2 \amp 4 \\ 0 \amp 2 \end{vmatrix} = 4
                </md>
                <md>
                    \det(A_2) = \begin{vmatrix} 7 \amp 2 \\ 3 \amp 0 \end{vmatrix} = -6
                </md>
                <md>
                    \vec{x} = \begin{bmatrix} \frac{\det(A_1)}{\det(A)} \\ \frac{\det(A_2)}{\det(A)} \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix} 2 \\ -3 \end{bmatrix}
                </md>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </problem>
</page>

<page>
    

    <problem workspace="2in">
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Show your work problem. Part of the grade is based on the justification/ work shown. Can have parts. E.g.
            </p>
        </introduction>
        <statement>
            <p>
                (5 points) Find an eigenvector <m>\vec{v}</m> of <m>A</m> corresponding to the eigenvalue <m>\lambda = 3</m>

                <md>
                    A = \begin{bmatrix} 7 \amp 2 \\ -2 \amp 2 \end{bmatrix}
                </md>
            </p>
            <sidebyside widths = "60% 40%">
            <p></p>
            <p>
                <md>
                    \vec{v} = \framebox{$\phantom{$\begin{bmatrix} a \amp a \amp a  \\ b \\ c \\ d \\ e  \end{bmatrix}$}$ }
                </md>
                
            </p>
            </sidebyside>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                An eigenvector <m>\vec{v}</m> corresponding to the eigenvalue <m>\lambda = 3</m> satisfies <m>(A - 3I)\vec{v} = \vec{0}</m>. Solving this system gives:
            </p>
            <p>
                <md>
                    (A - 3I) = \begin{bmatrix} 4 \amp 2 \\ -2 \amp -1 \end{bmatrix} \sim \begin{bmatrix} 2 \amp 1 \\ 0 \amp 0 \end{bmatrix}
                </md>
                
                and it is clear one nonzero vector in the null space of <m>\begin{bmatrix} 2 \amp 1 \\ 0 \amp 0 \end{bmatrix}</m> is <m>\vec{v} = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ -2 \end{bmatrix}</m>.
            </p>
        </solution>
        </problem>

        <problem workspace="2in">


        <statement>
            (3 points) Find the value of <m>h</m> such that the matrix

            <md>
                A = \begin{bmatrix}7 \amp h \\ 5 \amp -1 \end{bmatrix}
            </md>

            has eigenvalues <m>\lambda_1 = -3</m> and <m>\lambda_2 = 9</m>

            <md>
                h = \framebox{$\phantom{$\begin{bmatrix} a \amp a \amp a \end{bmatrix}$}$ }
            </md>
            
            
        </statement>

        <hint>
            <p>
                We want <m> \det(A - \lambda I) = 0</m> for the given eigenvalues.
            </p>
        </hint>
        <solution>
            <p>
                For <m> A </m> to have eigenvalues <m>\lambda_1 = -3</m> and <m>\lambda_2 = 9</m>, we must have:
                <md>
                    \det(A - \lambda_1 I) = 0 \quad \text{and} \quad \det(A - \lambda_2 I) = 0
                </md>
                Solving these equations gives:
                <md>
                    \det(A - (-3)I) = \det\begin{bmatrix} 10 \amp h \\ 5 \amp 2 \end{bmatrix} = 20 - 5h = 0 \quad \Rightarrow \quad h = 4
                </md>
                <md>
                    \det(A - 9I) = \det\begin{bmatrix} -2 \amp h \\ 5 \amp -10 \end{bmatrix} = 20 - 5h = 0 \quad \Rightarrow \quad h = 4
                </md>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </problem>




</page>
    
<page>
    <problem workspace="1.5in">
        <statement>
            <p> (6 points) <m> \textbf{Show your work for part (b) on this page.}</m>
                <md></md>
            
                Use the following Markov chain diagram to answer the questions.
                <md></md>
                (a) Find the stochastic matrix <m>P</m> of the Markov chain.

                <md></md>
                (b) Find the unique steady state probability vector <m>\vec{q}</m> of <m>P</m>
                <md></md>

                (c) If <m>\vec{x}_0 = \begin{pmatrix}10 \\ 18 \end{pmatrix}</m>, find the limit <m>l</m> of the sequence of vectors <m>P^k \vec{x}_0</m> as <m>k</m> tend to infinity.
                <md></md>
                

                <figure xml:id="markov-two-state">
  <image width="40%">
    <latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\begin{scope}[->,>=stealth',shorten >=1pt,auto,node distance=3cm,
  thick,main node/.style={circle,fill=blue!20,draw,font=\sffamily\Large\bfseries}]
   \node[main node] (1) {A};
   \node[main node] (2) [right of=1] {B};

   \path[every node/.style={font=\sffamily\small}]
      (1) edge [bend right] node[below] {0.3} (2)
          edge [loop above] node {0.7} (1)
      (2) edge node [above] {0.4} (1)
          edge [loop above] node {0.6} (2);
\end{scope}
\end{tikzpicture}
    </latex-image>
  </image>
</figure>
            </p>
            <sidebyside widths="50% 50%">
            <p></p>
            <p>

                <md>P = \framebox{$\phantom{$\begin{bmatrix} a \amp a \amp a \amp a \\ b \\ c \\ d \end{bmatrix}$}$ }</md>
                <md>\vec{q} = \framebox{$\phantom{$\begin{bmatrix} a \amp a \\ b  \\ c \\ d \\ e \end{bmatrix}$}$ }</md>
                <md>\vec{l} = \framebox{$\phantom{$\begin{bmatrix} a \amp a \\ b  \\ c \\ d \\ e \end{bmatrix}$}$ }</md>
            </p>
            </sidebyside>
            
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                By convention, the entry <m>P_{ij}</m> represents the probability of transitioning from state <m>j</m> to state <m>i</m>.
            </p>
            <p>
                <md>
                    P = \begin{bmatrix} 0.7 \amp 0.4 \\ 0.3 \amp 0.6 \end{bmatrix}
                </md>
                
            </p>
            <p>
                The unique steady state vector <m> \vec{q} </m> satisfies <m>P \vec{q} = \vec{q}</m>. Equivalently, <m>(P - I) \vec{q} = \vec{0}</m>.

                <md>
                    P - I = \begin{bmatrix} -0.3 \amp 0.4 \\ 0.3 \amp -0.4 \end{bmatrix} \sim \begin{bmatrix} 3 \amp -4 \\ 0 \amp 0 \end{bmatrix}
                </md>
                
                so one nonzero vector in <m>\Nul(P - I)</m> is <m>\vec{v} = \begin{bmatrix} 4 \\ 3 \end{bmatrix}</m>. But <m> \vec{q} </m> must be a probability vector, so we adjust <m>\vec{v}</m> to get
            </p>
            <p>
                <md>\vec{q} = \frac{1}{7} \begin{bmatrix} 4 \\ 3 \end{bmatrix}</md>

                Finally, to find <m> \vec{l} = \displaystyle \lim_{k \to \infty} P^k \vec{x}_o </m>, we use the steady state vector <m> \vec{q} </m> and get
                <md>
                    \vec{l} = \displaystyle \left( \sum_{i} x_i \right) \vec{q} = (10 + 18) \vec{q} = \begin{bmatrix} 16 \\ 12 \end{bmatrix}
                </md>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </problem>
</page>

<page>
    <problem workspace="1.5in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                (4 points) Show your work in the space below and put your answer in the boxes.

                Let

                <md>W = \left\{\begin{bmatrix} x_1 \\ x_2 \\ x_3 \\ x_4 \end{bmatrix} \in \mathbb{R}^4 \ : \ x_1 + 2x_2 - x_3 + 3x_4 = 0 \right\}.</md>
                Find a basis for <m>W</m> and state <m>\dim(W)</m>


                <md>
                    \text{basis for $W$} = \framebox{$\phantom{$\begin{bmatrix} a \amp a \amp a \amp a \amp a \amp a \amp a \amp a \amp a \\ b  \\ c  \\ d \\ e \\ f \\ g \\ h\end{bmatrix}$}$ }

                </md>
            </p>
            <sidebyside widths = "60% 40%">
            <p></p>
            <p>
                <md>
                    \dim(W) = \framebox{$\phantom{$\begin{bmatrix} a \amp a \\ b \end{bmatrix}$}$ }
                </md>
            </p>
            </sidebyside>
        </statement>
        <hint>
            <p>
                Write <m> W </m> as the span of some vectors.
            </p>
        </hint>
        <solution>
            <p>
                 <m> W </m> consists of all vectors <m> \vec{x} </m> satisfying the linear equation
                <md>
                    x_1 + 2x_2 - x_3 + 3x_4 = 0
                </md>
                In other words, for any vector <m> \vec{x} \in W </m>, we get
                <md>
                    <mrow>\vec{x} = \begin{bmatrix} x_1 \\ x_2 \\ x_3 \\ x_4 \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix} -2x_2 + x_3 - 3x_4 \\ x_2 \\ x_3 \\ x_4 \end{bmatrix}</mrow>

                    <mrow> = x_2 \begin{bmatrix} -2 \\ 1 \\ 0 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix} + x_3 \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 0 \\ 1 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix} + x_4 \begin{bmatrix} -3 \\ 0 \\ 0 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix} </mrow>
                </md>
                Therefore, a basis for <m> W </m> is
                <md>
                    \left\{ \begin{bmatrix} -2 \\ 1 \\ 0 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}, \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 0 \\ 1 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}, \begin{bmatrix} -3 \\ 0 \\ 0 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix} \right\}
                </md>
                and <m> \dim(W) = 3 </m>.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </problem>
</page>

<page>
    <problem workspace="1.5in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                (6 points) Show your work in the space below and put your answer in the boxes.

                Find the <m>LU</m> factorizations for the following matrix

                <md>
                    A = \begin{pmatrix} 1 \amp 2 \amp 1 \amp 3 \\ 2 \amp 5 \amp 2 \amp 4 \\ 3 \amp 4 \amp 3 \amp7 \end{pmatrix}
                </md>
            </p>
            <sidebyside widths = "50% 50%">
            <p></p>
            <p>
                <md>
                    L = \framebox{$\phantom{$\begin{bmatrix} a \amp a \amp a \amp a  \\ b  \\ c  \\ d\end{bmatrix}$}$ }
                </md>
                <md>
                    U = \framebox{$\phantom{$\begin{bmatrix} a \amp a \amp a \amp a  \\ b  \\ c  \\ d\end{bmatrix}$}$ }
                </md>
                
            </p>
            </sidebyside>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                We build the LU factorization step by step.
            </p>
            <p>
                <md>
                    A = \begin{pmatrix} 1 \amp 2 \amp 1 \amp 3 \\ 2 \amp 5 \amp 2 \amp 4 \\ 3 \amp 4 \amp 3 \amp7 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\  \amp 1 \amp 0 \\  \amp  \amp 1 \end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix} 1 \amp 2 \amp 1 \amp 3 \\ 2 \amp 5 \amp 2 \amp 4 \\ 3 \amp 4 \amp 3 \amp7 \end{pmatrix}
                </md>
                <md>
                    \overset{R_2 - 2R_1 \to R_2}{{\large \sim}} \begin{pmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\ 2 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\  \amp  \amp 1 \end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix} 1 \amp 2 \amp 1 \amp 3 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp -2 \\ 3 \amp 4 \amp 3 \amp7 \end{pmatrix}
                </md>
                <md>
                    \overset{R_3 - 3R_1 \to R_3}{{\large \sim}} \begin{pmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\ 2 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\ 3 \amp  \amp 1 \end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix} 1 \amp 2 \amp 1 \amp 3 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp -2 \\ 0 \amp -2 \amp 0 \amp -2 \end{pmatrix}
                </md>
                <md>
                    \overset{R_3 + 2R_2 \to R_3}{{\large \sim}} \begin{pmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\ 2 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\ 3 \amp -2 \amp 1 \end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix} 1 \amp 2 \amp 1 \amp 3 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp -2 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp -6 \end{pmatrix}
                </md>
            </p>
            <p>
                So the LU factorization of <m> A </m> is
                <md>
                    L = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\ 2 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\ 3 \amp -2 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix}, \quad U = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 2 \amp 1 \amp 3 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp -2 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp -6 \end{bmatrix}
                </md>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </problem>
</page>



</handout>